WHAT YOU MAY DO

  - Teach from these decks, in any setting, including commercially.
  - Copy and redistribute them in any medium or format.
  - Present them unmodified, with attribution.

You do not need to ask permission. Attribution is the only condition.


WHAT THE LICENSE DOES NOT ALLOW

  - Distributing a modified version. NoDerivatives means adaptations --
    re-cut, re-ordered, translated, restyled, or merged into another deck --
    may not be shared publicly.

  Modifying a copy for your own private use is not restricted; sharing the
  result is. If you want to distribute an adaptation, ask -- the project is
  generally happy to say yes, and would rather grant permission than have the
  material go unused.


HOW TO ATTRIBUTE

  Gray Roncal, W. (2026). "Introduction to Connectomics" (EN.585.781 Frontiers
  in Neuroengineering, Module 7). NeuroTrailblazers. CC BY-ND 4.0.
  https://neurotrailblazers.org/teaching/lectures/

  Substitute the relevant title and module number. A credit line on a title
  slide or in a syllabus is sufficient; you do not need to reproduce this file.


A NOTE ON THE CONTENTS

  These decks contain no third-party figures. They are text, tables, and
  ASCII diagrams, so redistributing them raises no image-licensing questions.

  They cite published work extensively. Citations are references, not
  reproductions -- the cited papers carry their own licenses, and nothing here
  grants any right over them. Where a deck quotes a reported figure or dataset
  statistic, that fact is attributed to its source on the slide or in the
  references slide.

  Externally sourced figures used elsewhere in this repository are catalogued
  with their individual licenses in assets/images/external/ATTRIBUTION.md.
  None of them appear in these decks.


NO WARRANTY

  Provided as-is. The material describes a field that moves quickly; figures,
  dataset sizes, and "state of the field" claims are accurate as of the last
  revision date on each deck and will age. Verify before relying on a specific
  number in your own work.
